<chapter>

<title><classname>HttpdOpSys</classname> Reference</title>
 <section><title>Introduction</title>

  <para>
   <classname>HttpdOpSys</classname> serves as an abstraction layer
   between Seminole's platform-independent code and the specific
   interfaces offered by the host operating system.  Primarily, it
   provides generic memory and process management facilities, since
   these are the most basic requirements for Seminole.
  </para>  

  <para>
   More detailed architectural discussion of Seminole's portability
   mechanisms can be found in <xref linkend="portingOpEnv">.
  </para>

  <para>
   When requesting services from the underlying host operating system
   through <classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>, it is possible for
   internal errors to eventually be returned to the caller within
   Seminole.  However, these errors are abstracted to generic
   equivalents which are descriptive of the error condition, but do
   not depend on any platform-specific representation.  The possible
   <acronym>OS</acronym> errors are listed in <xref
   linkend="opsErrorCodes">.
  </para>

  <table id="opsErrorCodes" frame=top rowsep=0 tocentry=1>
  <title>OS Abstraction Layer Error Codes</title>
  <tgroup cols=2 colsep=1>
  <thead>
   <row>
    <entry>Constant</entry>
    <entry>Meaning</entry>
   </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
   <row>
    <entry>ERR_NOTFOUND</entry>
    <entry>File, directory, or entity not found</entry>
   </row>
   <row>
    <entry>ERR_SYSPERM</entry>
    <entry>Administrative permission denied</entry>
   </row>
   <row>
    <entry>ERR_NOTREADY</entry>
    <entry>Device, resource, or unit not ready</entry>
   </row>
   <row>
    <entry>ERR_LIMITRCHD</entry>
    <entry>Maximum limit or capacity reached</entry>
   </row>
   <row>
    <entry>ERR_IO</entry>
    <entry>Low-level or hardware I/O error</entry>
   </row>
   <row>
    <entry>ERR_WRONGTYPE</entry>
    <entry>Inappropriate type or target for operation</entry>
   </row>
   <row>
    <entry>ERR_OUTOFMEM</entry>
    <entry>Ran out of memory</entry>
   </row>
   <row>
    <entry>ERR_BADPARAM</entry>
    <entry>Invalid or out-of-range parameter</entry>
   </row>
   <row>
    <entry>ERR_BADFORMAT</entry>
    <entry>The provided data is corrupted or not in a valid format.</entry>
   </row>
   <row>
    <entry>ERR_UNKNOWN</entry>
    <entry>Unknown/untranslatable error</entry>
   </row>
  </tbody>
  </tgroup>
  </table>

 </section>

 <section><title>Public Methods</title>

  <section id="opsMalloc" xreflabel="HttpdOpSys::Malloc">
   <title><function>Malloc</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       void
       *<function><classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>::Malloc</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>size_t <parameter>sz</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Allocate new memory <parameter class=function>sz</parameter> bytes
    in length.
   </para>

   <para>
    Returns a pointer to a buffer of at least the requested size,
    taking into account host alignment requirements, or
    <symbol>NULL</symbol> upon error.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section id="opsFree" xreflabel="HttpdOpSys::Free">
  <title><function>Free</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       void
       <function><classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>::Free</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>void *<parameter>p_ptr</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Release a block of allocated memory pointed to by <parameter
    class=function>p_ptr</parameter>.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>Realloc</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       void
       *<function><classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>::Realloc</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>void *<parameter>p_oldptr</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>size_t <parameter>newsz</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Expand or shrink the size of the memory block pointed to by <parameter
    class=function>p_oldptr</parameter>, to be <parameter
    class=function>newsz</parameter> bytes in length.
   </para>

   <para>
    Returns a revised pointer upon success, or <symbol>NULL</symbol>
    upon failure.
   </para>

   <important>
    <para>
     If <function>Realloc()</function> fails to change the size of a
     given block of memory, the original block is invalidated and
     cannot be used.
    </para>
   </important>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>Fork</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       bool
       <function><classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>::Fork
        </function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>void (*p_func)(HttpdParameter p1, HttpdParameter p2,
      HttpdParameter p3)</paramdef>
      <paramdef>HttpdParameter <parameter>p1</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>HttpdParameter <parameter>p2</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>HttpdParameter <parameter>p3</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Create a new process, job, or task (depending on the host
    platform), which will immediately enter the function <parameter
    class=function>p_func</parameter>, passing it <parameter
    class=function>p1</parameter>, <parameter
    class=function>p2</parameter>, and <parameter
    class=function>p3</parameter> as arguments.
   </para>

   <para>
    Returns <returnvalue>true</returnvalue> upon successful process
    creation, <returnvalue>false</returnvalue> upon failure.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section>
  <title><function>JoinAll</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       void
       <function><classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>::JoinAll</function>
      </funcdef><void>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    This method is called after <classname>Httpd</classname> ceases to
    <function>Fork()</function> new threads, in order to wait for
    existing threads to die.  This is done to ensure internal
    consistency of the handler chain.
   </para>

   <para>
    When <function>JoinAll()</function> returns, all subthreads have
    completed their processing and exited.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section>
  <title><function>NativeFileSystem</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       HttpdFileSystem *
       <function><classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>::NativeFileSystem</function>
      </funcdef><void>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    Some operating systems have their own native file systems (well, most
    actually). On these systems, the native filesystem is abstracted
    as a <xref linkend="fsAbstract"> interface 
    (<classname>HttpdFileSystem</classname>).
   </para>
   <para>
    On operating systems that do not have a native file system available
    this routine shall return <symbol>NULL</symbol>. 
   </para>
  </section>

  <section id="OpenSystemFile" xreflabel="HttpdOpSys::OpenSystemFile">
  <title><function>OpenSystemFile</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       int
       <function><classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>::OpenSystemFile</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>const char *<parameter>p_filename</parameter></paramdef>
      <paramdef>HttpdDataSource *&<parameter>p_source</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
    This routine maps a native operating system file to a 
    <xref linkend="HttpdDataSource"> abstraction.
   </para>
   <para>
    The address of the created data source object is placed in 
    <parameter>p_source</parameter>. When the object is no longer
    needed it should be released with <xref linkend="CloseSystemFile">.
   </para>
   <para>
    Upon success, <literal>0</literal> is returned; otherwise a system
    dependent error value is returned (see <xref
    linkend="opsErrorCodes">).
   </para>
   <note>
    <para>
     This routine is optional and does not have to be implemented. 
     If implemented, the symbol HTTPD_HAVE_NATIVE_FILE_SOURCES
     should be defined. 
    </para>
   </note>   
  </section>

  <section id="CloseSystemFile" xreflabel="HttpdOpSys::CloseSystemFile">
  <title><function>CloseSystemFile</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       void
       <function><classname>HttpdOpSys</classname>::CloseSystemFile</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>HttpdDataSource *<parameter>p_source</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>
   <para>
    This releases the file mapping created with 
    <xref linkend="OpenSystemFile">. The data source pointed to by 
    <parameter>p_source</parameter> is no longer valid after this
    method is called.
   </para>
  </section> 

 </section>

 <section><title>Public Data</title>

  <para>
   <classname>HttpdOpSys</classname> contains no publically
   accessible data members.
  </para>

 </section>

</chapter>

